## BEGIN DESCRIPTION
## Trig functions defined by point on the terminal side
## END DESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Trigonometry)
## DBchapter(Trigonometric functions)
## DBsection(Sine & cosine functions - definitions, graphs, & properties)
## Date(January 2012)
## Institution(Mississippi College)
## Author(John Travis)
## MO(1)
## Level(2)
## TitleText1('Trigonometry')
## KEYWORDS('trig function', 'definition', 'terminal', 'standard')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$a = Real(non_zero_random(-10, 10));
$b = Real(non_zero_random(-10, 10));
$c = Compute("sqrt(($a)^2+($b)^2)");

##   values of trig functions
$sin = $b / $c;
$cos = $a / $c;
$tan = $b / $a;
$cot = $a / $b;
$sec = $c / $a;
$csc = $c / $b;

$max = max(abs($a), abs($b));

$plot = Plot(
    xmin => -$max - 1,
    xmax => $max + 1,
    ymin => -$max - 1,
    ymax => $max + 1
);
$plot->add_point($a, $b, color => 'red');
$plot->add_dataset([ 0, 0 ], [ $a, $b ], end_mark => 'arrow');
$plot->add_arc([ 0, 0 ], [ 0.5, 0 ], [ $a, $b ], width => 1);
$plot->add_label(
    0.5, 0, '\\(\\alpha\\)',
    v_align => 'bottom',
    h_align => 'left'
);

BEGIN_PGML
An angle [`\alpha`] in standard position has a terminal side which passes through the point [` ([$a],[$b]) `]. 

>> [!A arrow from (0,0) to ([$a],[$b]). A arc from the positive x-axis counterclockwise to the arrow is drawn and labled "alpha".!]{$plot}{250}<<

Determine values for the following:

a) [`\sin(\alpha)`]  = [_]{$sin}

b) [`\cos(\alpha)`]  = [_]{$cos}

c) [`\tan(\alpha)`]  = [_]{$tan}

d) [`\cot(\alpha)`]  = [_]{$cot}

e) [`\sec(\alpha)`]  = [_]{$sec}

f) [`\csc(\alpha)`]  = [_]{$csc}

END_PGML
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Given the data point [` ([$a],[$b]) `] on the terminal side of the angle, use the Pythagorean Theorem.

Distance from the origin along the terminal side is 
[``` \sqrt{([$a])^2+([$b])^2} = \sqrt{[$a**2+$b**2]} \approx [$c] ```]  
Plugging into the definition:

[``\sin(\alpha) = \frac{[$b]}{[$c]} = [$sin]``]

[``\cos(\alpha) = \frac{[$a]}{[$c]} = [$cos]``]

[``\tan(\alpha) = \frac{[$b]}{[$a]} = [$tan]``]

[``\cot(\alpha) = \frac{[$a]}{[$b]} = [$cot]``]

[``\sec(\alpha) = \frac{[$c]}{[$a]} = [$sec]``]

[``\csc(\alpha) = \frac{[$c]}{[$b]} = [$csc]``]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT(); 